
 Refresh this page on every visit to ensure you get the latest information.

 NOTE - this README is also available online at the following URL :

   https://www.langelaar.net

 WARNING - only apply these fixes to JNOS 2.0q - nothing earlier

 The latest official version 2.0q was released on August 16, 2025

 How to update your JNOS 2.0q sources, simply overlay new code on top :

   rsync -av www.langelaar.net::development < official src area >

   cd < official src area >

   make

 Note : you will rarely need to do the full clean, configure, and make

 -----------------

 * 06Aug2026, Thursday

  Prototype code to allow JNOS to drop incoming traffic, where the source
  ip address is covered by an external IPSET list maintained on the linux
  side. I have such a list that protects my web server, and I see attacks
  from the same intruders directed towards JNOS all the time.

  I find the IPSET lists easy and flexible, so why not allow JNOS to use
  them as well, via the IPSET libraries ? This seems to be working quite
  well, not sure how resource or system sensitive/friendly this is, but
  so far it's doing what I want it to do. At the end of the day, I want
  to have multiple lists configured in autoexec.nos, for example, wider
  scope allow list, followed by a drop list to prune things down, etc.

   one new file, j2ipset.c, and one modified file, iproute.c

  Edit makefile, add '-DJ2IPSET' to end of PATCHES directive, add j2ipset.o
   to the end of the NET= ojbect file section, and add -lipset to the end of
    the LCURSES = line, then enter 'make' to compile.
 
  Make sure you have ipset libraries (development) installed :]

  You might want to edit iproute.c, comment out -> log (-1, "deny [%s]" ...

  Concept project, I will be putting all of this stuff together in the next
  few weeks, into a new release, including the ax25 v2.2 code - no more tar
  files, or loose files just getting thrown into the REPO. I have been lax
  lately, mostly because I've had little time to work on this stuff, but I
  want to get these ideas out to the masses incase I get hit by a bus, or
  something worse.

 * 05Aug2026, Wednesday

  I have rudamentary XID support working by the looks of it. Direwolf sends me
  an XID and I reply with an XID, based on my own ax25 parameters. Code needs
  some cleanup, and improvement, but it's 'working' to my expectations :]

   1 new file modification -> lapb.c

  TO BE CLEAR -> JNOS is using a KISS TNC, not direwolf. The AX.25 V2.2 is
   entirely being done within the JNOS ax25 stack. I am only using direwolf
    as the remote AX.25 V2.2 station, which connects to my JNOS system.

 * 04Aug2026, Tuesday

  Trace can now properly decode ax25 v2.2 :

   2 new files modified -> ax25dump.c, trace.c

  Use new 'trace ax25ver' command to toggle version - default is old version

  Note : since the ax25dump functions are stateless (?) you have to tell it
   which version of ax25 you want it to decode, using the new command.

  Unless I've misread the code, the proto field only gives major, not minor.
   (so this is a quick and dirty way to deal with it - for now anyways)

  It's NOT perfect. The problem is if you toggle to v2.2, then any v2.0
  traffic isn't 'properly decoded', and if you toogle back to v2.0, the
  same thing, v2.2 isn't 'properly decoded'. It's not ideal, but at the
  end of the day, the hexdump is the same. I will figure something out.

 * 27Jul2026

  I think I have it working - basic support for incoming AX.25 V2.2

  New tar file : jnos_ax25_v2.2_patch.3.tar
   [ same instructions as below, for 11Jul2026 ]

  JNOS autoexec (just the modem attach) :

    attach asy soundmodem0 - ax25 sm0 8192 512 1200
    trace sm0 0x211 sm0.log 1000

  New file : soundmodem.conf [ the test file I used on my system ]

  My test environment :

  JNOS is attached to soundmodem (Thomas Sailer) in KISS mode, and I use
  QtTermTCP64 as a client attached to direwolf. Both modems are talking
  to each other through the ALSA loopback module (snd-aloop), creates
  virtual, interconnected soundcards in Linux.

  Direwolf config (modem entry) :

    DEVICE  plughw:2,1

  This is VERY VERY experimental, connected sessions appear to stall out
  the odd time, not sure why yet, but basic tests suggests it's working.

 * 11Jul2026

   Post Note : seeing a minor problem already UA is sending an extra
    byte, which causes direwolf to send another SABME, ugggg ...

  New tar file : jnos_ax25_v2.2_patch.2.tar

    cd < jnos source area > ; tar xvf jnos_ax25_v2.2_patch.2.tar

    edit makefile, added '-DAX25_V22_Sep2017' to end of PATCHES directive

    make clean; configure; make

   THIS IS VERY EXPERIMENTAL - structure changes to accommodate AX25 V2.2
     (support for SABME, modulo 128, no support for XID or SREJ yet)

   Don't count on trace to give accurate control information (yet), but
   the hex dumps themselves should be good enough, you can clearly see
   the control field is 2 bytes nows for I and S frames.

   Note : the focus of this code is incoming sessions ...

 * 10Jul2026

  I turned 61 today - grateful to be alive :]

 * 06Jul2026

  FORGOT TO SAY : Do not update with these, do not recompile, this is just
    an evolving backup of my work, a reference, incase I get hit by bus

  Making good progress, after putting together a linux test setup :

   slackware 15.0 (home development system)
   jnos kiss interface to thomas sailer soundmodem <-> /dev/dsp2
   using the alsa snd_aloop virtual audio bridge
   QtTermTCP64 (agw setup) to direwolf <-> ADEVICE plughw:2,1

  Backup of latest AX25 V2.2 code is 'jnos_ax25_v2.2_patch.tar' :]

  I don't think I got the sequence numbers quite right yet ...

 * 24Jun2026

   Started work on AX25 Version 2.2 (1998) - get that going first,
    before even thinking about looking at Sep 2017 version 2.2

   Do not update with these, do not recompile, just letting people know
    (an evolving backup of my work, incase I get hit by a bus)

     added 'char modulo' to ax25_cb flags structure - to flag 8 or 128

     added -DAX25_V22_Sep2017 end of PATCHES in makefile

     some new definitions required for Version 2.2 :

      #define SREJ 0x13
      #define SABME 0x6f
      #define PF128 0x100
      #define MMASK128 127

   Doing this from scratch - no AX25 addendum, so comparing AX25 specs ...

 * 18Apr2026

   fbbfwd.c - I was not looking for the ';PM:' comments when we do a proposal
   to the Winlink CMS server, and there happens to be messages waiting for us
   on the CMS side, resulting in the dread 'expected FS' error.

   This update should fix the issue.

   Before this fix, one could work around the problem by forcing a reverse
   forward session with 'mbox ki Wl2k', which immediately gets any messages
   waiting for you on CMS side. Note use of capitalized 'W' to do this.

 * 15Nov2025

   forward.c - you can now configure a fallback connection for a particular
   area, in case the primary connection fails, or times out on you. This is
   very easy to implement. Note the use of a new 'ALT' directive in two of
   the examples shown below :

    1) if connect over vhf port fails or times out, try internet wormhole

      ---------
      ve4pkt
      c vhf ve4pkt ALT c ax0 ve4pkt-1
      ve4pkt
      ww
      --------

    2) if you can't connect direct, then try using a digipeater instead

      c vhf ve4pkt ALT c vhf ve4pkt digi1 ... digiN

